1
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Argonne National Laboratory's Idaho Division,

2
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
located at the National Reactor Testing Station,

3
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
operates the experimental breeder Reactor 1.

4
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Experience gained since 1951 in the operation of this reactor

5
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
forms the foundation for fast power reactor development.

6
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
This is the first reactor to employ sodium-potassium alloy as a coolant

7
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
and to utilize the direct current electromagnetic pump.

8
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
During this time, the reactor performed satisfactorily as a steady power source,

9
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
demonstrated the breeding concept, generated the world's first electricity from nuclear power,

10
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
and served its primary function in providing fundamental fast reactor information.

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,000
When EBR-1 was operated at power-to-flow ratios one and one-half times,

12
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
the design ratio, some undesirable dynamic characteristics were observed. These were

13
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:38,000
a prompt positive temperature coefficient and a tendency toward spontaneous oscillation

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,000
of power. It was believed these characteristics were related and that the cause of the prompt

15
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:48,000
positive temperature coefficient was either the Doppler effect, which increases the effective

16
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
fissioning cross-section due to heating enriched uranium, or to mechanical motion of the fuel

17
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,000
elements. Enriched uranium in the form of the helix was prepared for measurement of the Doppler

18
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:07,000
effect in fast reactors. This experiment was performed in the laboratory's critical assembly

19
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:15,000
facility, ZPR-3. The helical form was adopted to minimize motion due to heating, which could

20
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,000
produce undesirable side reactivity effects. Positioned within an assembled EBR-1 core

21
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
mock-up the helix was periodically heated then cooled on a 20 second cycle time

22
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
careful measurement of the power oscillations resulting from the cycling

23
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
made it fairly certain the doppler effect was much too small

24
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
to have been the primary cause of the prompt temperature coefficient observed in the reactor

25
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
concluding that the primary problem was mechanical motion it was decided to investigate ebr1

26
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
performance as part of an overall program to study reactor dynamics.

27
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
A rigid core assembly to restrict mechanical motion was designed for EBR-1.

28
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Designated Mark III, the core structure consisting of laminated steel disks was machined and

29
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
assembled by the laboratory's central shops.

30
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
The inside hexagonal surface fits the outer perimeter of the blanket sub-assemblies.

31
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
These discs are held and aligned with tie rods.

32
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Paralleling the assembly of the core structure, fabrication of Mark III fuel was underway by the metallurgy division.

33
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
The fuel for the Mark III core is uranium enriched to 93%.

34
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
The blanket is normal uranium.

35
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Both blanket and core uranium are alloyed with 2 weight percent zirconium.

36
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Clad with zircaloy 2, they are co-extruded into the form of a rod.

37
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,000
The rough cores for the blanket extrusion billets are produced by casting in a multi-cavity

38
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:57,000
graphite mold.

39
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
The enriched uranium is melted in a 5-kilogram batch and cast in a single-cavity mold.

40
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
A graphite crucible, coated with ceramic wash, is charged with the uranium biscuit and zirconium

41
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
sponge for the blanket alloy.

42
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Melting and alloying is done by induction heating in a vacuum of ten

43
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:29,000
microns or less. After alloying the metal is bottom poured into the mold by

44
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:39,000
lifting a stopper rod. These castings after machining are tested for

45
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
homogeneity by a non-destructive method. A sound transmission principle is

46
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,000
utilized. Five mega cycle ultrasonic waves are generated by means of a quartz

47
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:55,000
transducer and sent through the metal. The quality of the casting is

48
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:04,000
graphically recorded on a strip of electrosensitive paper. The casting, after

49
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:09,000
further machining, yields two extrusion billet cores. These cores, assembled with

50
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:18,000
copper nickel end plugs are encased in a sleeve of Zercaloy II. A copper jacket is added to

51
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,000
prevent contamination of the billet components during heating and to provide lubrication

52
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:38,000
during extrusion. Closed by Heliarc welding, the assembled billet is then evacuated, outgassed

53
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,000
and sealed.

54
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:49,000
The billets are co-extruded by nuclear metals incorporated to rods of one-half inch diameter.

55
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Upon return, they are inspected, pickled to remove the copper, and swaged to a specified

56
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
diameter.

57
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Heat treated for stabilization, the rods are cut to appropriate fuel and blanket lengths,

58
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
and the cladding thickness measured.

59
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
To join the sections of either type rod, a commercial glass lathe was specifically adapted

60
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
at Argonne for tungsten arc butt welding.

61
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,000
This welding operation is extremely precise because of the difficulty of fusing the high

62
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:29,000
melting zircaloy without excessive melting of the uranium.

63
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:34,000
A 10-mil zirconium spacer disc separates the enriched and blanket sections on each fuel rod.

64
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Chill and gas retainers surround the electrode and weld area.

65
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Welding is purposely limited to the 20-mil zircaloy cladding and is made in a double pass,

66
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
each pass requiring a different welding current.

67
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:08,000
A section bond is made at each weld by fusing the metal in a small zone to add mechanical

68
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:18,000
strength.

69
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:22,000
The cladding is protected against oxidation by passing a stream of helium through the

70
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Vicor II.

71
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:34,000
After heat treatment, cladding thickness is measured by an eddy-current method.

72
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Eddy currents are induced in the Zircaloy cladding by means of a point probe.

73
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:46,000
The apparent impedance of the probe is influenced by the cladding thickness.

74
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:53,000
The bond on the rods is then scanned by the ultrasonic transmission technique.

75
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
testing methods help to ensure the quality of the completed fuel and blanket rods.

76
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,000
Zirconium ribs are spot welded to rods ground to their finished diameter.

77
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:12,000
These will provide accurate spacing of the tightly clustered rods in their final geometry.

78
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
The welding operation is performed automatically on a specially designed

79
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:26,000
condenser discharge type spot welder. Welds are placed at one quarter inch intervals along each

80
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:35,000
of the three ribs, a total of 240 welds per rod. Machining operations complete the fuel and blanket

81
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:45,000
rod fabrication. The completed core structure ready for installation in the reactor tank

82
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
has a total height of nine feet and five inches.

83
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:55,000
The reduced diameter at the bottom will receive the core and inner blanket rods.

84
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:01,000
The structure rests on this shoulder with the inlet plenum chamber located just above the upper blanket.

85
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:08,000
This plenum chamber contains four two-way inlet valves for switching from series to parallel flow.

86
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
The up position of the valves permits series flow through the inner blanket and core,

87
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
while the down position allows parallel flow.

88
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:25,000
The plate above the inlet plenum chamber has seal rings to prevent bypass leakage between the inlet and outlet plenum chambers.

89
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:34,000
This seal plate also contains two flow control valves for throttling the exit flow from the inner blanket under parallel flow conditions.

90
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Under series flow conditions, these valves are closed.

91
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Additional rigidity is obtained by an arrangement of clamps around fuel and blanket sub-assemblies

92
00:09:48,000 --> 00:10:04,000
at the core center line and at the seal plate.

93
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
The upper part of the structure is primarily shielding with an overflow plenum chamber

94
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
near the top.

95
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:15,000
The top plate holds the actuators and position indicators for the various valves and clamps

96
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:23,000
in the structure.

97
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:28,000
The core structure is installed in the same reactor tank that housed the EBR-1, Mark I

98
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Mk. II cores. Fuel and blanket rods, with their attached extensions for handling, are

99
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:55,000
positioned within a hexagonal can. Complete sub-assemblies within the hex can contain

100
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:59,000
36 rods, and depending upon location within the structure,

101
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
will consist of fuel and or blanket material.

102
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
A tightening rod is located at the center of each sub-assembly

103
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
and, when expanded, provides additional rigidity.

104
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
The thorough temperature instrumentation of Mark III

105
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
includes thermocouples in the plenum chamber structure,

106
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
fuel rods, blanket rods, and coolant passages.

107
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
The complete Mark III loading contains 19 sub-assemblies.

108
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Seven enriched sub-assemblies comprise the core.

109
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
These are surrounded by a ring of 12 inner blanket sub-assemblies.

110
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:49,000
Criticality was achieved on November 11, 1957.

111
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:55,000
It was determined to be 47 and 4 tenths kilograms of uranium-235,

112
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:00,000
within 2% of the critical mass determined from the mock-up of the core

113
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
on the zero-power reactor 3.

114
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
Operating test data indicates that Mark 3 would be stable

115
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:15,000
at several times design power, and the instability apparent in Mark 2 was due to mechanical design

116
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
and is not inherent in fast reactors.

117
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
This investigation of reactor dynamics is continuing

118
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:28,000
and will establish parameters affecting the stability of fast reactors.
